From f20963794dbf2cb4c2ae84720a098c2782d1533b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 00:03:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: the link set up again after a hot-plug event
 read from what nvidia-drm says, and SCDC read off the thread that draws,
 through the connector's own DDC port

Found by an independent review of 1.0 RC6, and reproduced on the host:

- an output on FRL (3840x2160 at 119.88 Hz on an RTX 4080 SUPER to an LG
  C5, the GUI that the user keeps) was turned off and on after any hot-plug
  event: TMDS_Config (SCDC 0x20) says nothing of an FRL link, and every mode
  above 340 MHz was taken as scrambled TMDS. Over FRL that is 3 to 6 s of
  black, and nvidia-drm refused to turn such an output on again (-22) in a
  test of 1.0 RC2;
- a mode sent in YCbCr 4:2:0 (3840x2160 at 60 Hz, 8 bits: 297 MHz, not
  scrambled) was counted at its full rate, and its TMDS_Config of 0 taken as
  a display that lost its setup;
- the adapter of the connector was looked for on the thread that draws,
  before the commit of the frame, by reading the EDID address of every i2c
  adapter of the card, buses that are not the display's among them (the RGB
  controller of a board).

Now:

- the link is what nvidia-drm says (NV_OUTPUT_LINK: TMDS with or without
  scrambling, FRL at its rate, DisplayPort, and the pixel format; LinkOf()).
  TMDS without scrambling and DisplayPort need nothing written. Over FRL the
  display kept its setup where Config_1 (SCDC 0x31) has an FRL_Rate other than
  0; over scrambled TMDS where TMDS_Config has scrambling and the 1/40 ratio
  on (SinkOf()). Where the driver does not say the link, a mode is counted at
  its TMDS rate in its format (4:2:2 at its pixel clock, 4:2:0 at half; RGB
  where the format is not known), and an FRL_Rate other than 0 is a kept FRL
  link;
- SCDC is read on a thread of its own (std::async), and the frame never waits
  for it: the verdict is made when the read answers, with the output as it is
  then. Only the adapter of the DDC port that nvidia-drm names for the
  connector (NV_DDC_I2C_PORT) is opened, found by its name in sysfs; no other
  bus of the card is touched. That it is the connector's port is checked by the
  EDID that it gives. Where nvidia-drm does not name a port, or the read fails,
  the output is turned off and on as before, and kodi.log says why.

kodi.log gives the link with the verdict ("... over FRL (pixel clock ... kHz,
8 bpc)", "SCDC of the display, read through i2c-N (the DDC port P of the
connector), the link FRL: TMDS_Config 0x00, Config_1 0x05, ...: the display has
the setup of the link").

The decision stays in HotplugRelink.h, without DRM, for a host test.
---
 xbmc/windowing/gbm/HotplugRelink.h            | 221 ++++++++---
 .../windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp | 365 +++++++++++-------
 xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.h  |  19 +-
 3 files changed, 416 insertions(+), 189 deletions(-)

diff --git a/xbmc/windowing/gbm/HotplugRelink.h b/xbmc/windowing/gbm/HotplugRelink.h
index 0a0e5c3..54feb51 100644
--- a/xbmc/windowing/gbm/HotplugRelink.h
+++ b/xbmc/windowing/gbm/HotplugRelink.h
@@ -11,39 +11,45 @@
 #include <algorithm>
 #include <cstdint>
 #include <optional>
+#include <string>
 #include <utility>
 #include <vector>
 
 /*
- * Hades: a display that comes back on an HDMI link that the source scrambles, and that nvidia-drm
- * does not set up again.
+ * Hades: a display that comes back on an HDMI link that the source scrambles or trains, and that
+ * nvidia-drm does not set up again.
  *
  * Above a TMDS character rate of 340 MHz (HDMI 2.0) the source scrambles the link and tells the
- * sink so through its SCDC registers (TMDS_Config: scrambling on, bit clock ratio 1/40). A sink
- * that is switched off and on resets those registers, and gives a hot-plug pulse. Until the source
- * writes them again the sink cannot read the link: no picture, while the source and its state are
- * as before. i915 reads TMDS_Config at each hot-plug event and sets the link up again where the
- * sink lost it (intel_hdmi_reset_link(), intel_ddi.c), and amdgpu does a commit for a sink it saw
- * leave (dm_restore_drm_connector_state(); linux-9964 for one that it did not see leave). NVKMS
- * writes them in the closed part of the driver, only when it sets a head up: after such a pulse
- * nvidia-drm sends the event, reads the same EDID and does nothing more. Issue #11 (RTX 3050, LG
- * B7, HDMI-A-3, 3840x2160 at 50 Hz, 594 MHz, scrambled): no picture after the TV was switched off
- * and on; the kernel's log and the DRM state the same before and after; a change to 1080p (not
- * scrambled) brought the picture back.
+ * sink so through its SCDC registers (TMDS_Config: scrambling on, bit clock ratio 1/40); over FRL
+ * (HDMI 2.1) the source trains the link at the rate that it writes there (Config_1: FRL_Rate). A
+ * sink that is switched off and on resets those registers, and gives a hot-plug pulse. Until the
+ * source writes them again the sink cannot read the link: no picture, while the source and its
+ * state are as before. i915 reads TMDS_Config at each hot-plug event and sets the link up again
+ * where the sink lost it (intel_hdmi_reset_link(), intel_ddi.c), and amdgpu does a commit for a
+ * sink it saw leave (dm_restore_drm_connector_state(); linux-9964 for one that it did not see
+ * leave). NVKMS writes them in the closed part of the driver, only when it sets a head up: after
+ * such a pulse nvidia-drm sends the event, reads the same EDID and does nothing more. Issue #11
+ * (RTX 3050, LG B7, HDMI-A-3, 3840x2160 at 50 Hz, 594 MHz, scrambled): no picture after the TV was
+ * switched off and on; the kernel's log and the DRM state the same before and after; a change to
+ * 1080p (not scrambled) brought the picture back.
  *
  * So, on nvidia-drm, a hot-plug event after which the display in use is there, with the output on
- * in a mode whose link is scrambled, has the output turned off and on again once, as when the
- * screen is blanked and woken: NVKMS sets the head and the link up from scratch, SCDC included,
- * only where the display lost the setup. Kodi reads TMDS_Config of the display itself, as i915 does,
- * through the i2c adapter that the NVIDIA kernel module registers for the DDC port of the connector
- * (nv-i2c.c: one adapter per I2C port of the GPU, whose transfers its resource manager makes under
- * its own locks, as its reads of the EDID; which one is the connector's is found by the EDID it
- * gives: AdapterOfEdid()). Read only: the offset of a register, then its byte. Where it cannot be
- * read (no adapter, none or two of them giving the display's EDID, a refused transfer) the output
- * is turned off and on as when it is not known whether the display lost it: a picture that was
- * there goes black once, for the time the TV takes to sync again. It is not done:
- * - where the link is not scrambled (at most 340 MHz: the sink needs nothing written);
- * - where the display kept the setup (TMDS_Config has scrambling and the 1/40 ratio on);
+ * in a mode whose link is scrambled or trained, has the output turned off and on again once, as
+ * when the screen is blanked and woken: NVKMS sets the head and the link up from scratch, SCDC
+ * included, only where the display lost the setup. What the link is, nvidia-drm says
+ * (NV_OUTPUT_LINK: TMDS with or without scrambling, FRL at its rate, the pixel format: LinkOf());
+ * where it does not, it is told from the mode (TmdsKHz()). Kodi reads the display's SCDC itself,
+ * as i915 does, through the i2c adapter that the NVIDIA kernel module registers for the DDC port of
+ * the connector, which nvidia-drm names (NV_DDC_I2C_PORT; nv-i2c.c: one adapter per I2C port of the
+ * GPU, whose transfers its resource manager makes under its own locks, as its reads of the EDID),
+ * off the thread that draws, and no other bus of the card; that the port is the connector's is
+ * checked by the EDID that it gives (AdapterOfEdid()). Read only: the offset of a register, then
+ * its byte. Where it cannot be read the output is turned off and on as when it is not known whether
+ * the display lost it. It is not done:
+ * - where the link is neither scrambled nor trained (TMDS at 340 MHz or less, a mode in YCbCr
+ *   4:2:0 counted at its own rate: the sink needs nothing written), or is DisplayPort;
+ * - where the display kept the setup (TMDS_Config with scrambling and the 1/40 ratio on; over FRL,
+ *   an FRL_Rate other than 0);
  * - where the event is explained by another output of the device, which changed (connected,
  *   disconnected, or another EDID) while the one in use did not, unless the display in use is
  *   known to have lost the setup;
@@ -64,47 +70,140 @@ constexpr int64_t AFTER_OWN_MS = 10000;
 constexpr int64_t SPACING_MS = 30000;
 //! HDMI 2.0: above this TMDS character rate the source scrambles the link
 constexpr int64_t SCRAMBLED_ABOVE_KHZ = 340000;
+//! ... and above this one TMDS cannot carry it: HDMI 2.1 sends it over FRL
+constexpr int64_t TMDS_MAX_KHZ = 600000;
 
-//! The DDC addresses and SCDC registers that are read (HDMI 2.0)
+//! The DDC addresses and SCDC registers that are read (HDMI 2.0 and 2.1)
 constexpr uint8_t EDID_ADDRESS = 0x50;
 constexpr uint8_t SCDC_ADDRESS = 0x54;
 constexpr uint8_t SCDC_TMDS_CONFIG = 0x20; //!< bit 0 Scrambling_Enable, bit 1 TMDS_Bit_Clock_Ratio
 constexpr uint8_t SCDC_SCRAMBLER_STATUS = 0x21; //!< bit 0 Scrambling_Status
+constexpr uint8_t SCDC_CONFIG_1 = 0x31; //!< bits 3:0 FRL_Rate (0: TMDS), bits 7:4 FFE_Levels
 constexpr uint8_t SCDC_STATUS_FLAGS_0 = 0x40; //!< bit 0 Clock_Detected, bits 1-3 Ch0-2_Locked
+//! nvidia-drm's NV_DDC_I2C_PORT where the connector has no DDC port that it knows
+constexpr uint64_t NO_DDC_PORT = 255;
+
+//! What the link of the output is (NV_OUTPUT_LINK of nvidia-drm, LinkOf())
+enum class Link
+{
+  UNKNOWN, //!< not said (a driver without it, or nothing driven)
+  TMDS, //!< TMDS without scrambling
+  TMDS_SCRAMBLED, //!< TMDS above 340 MHz, or scrambled at less where the display asks for it
+  FRL, //!< HDMI 2.1 FRL
+  DP, //!< DisplayPort
+};
+
+//! The pixel format of the output (NV_OUTPUT_LINK, bits 16-23)
+enum class Format
+{
+  RGB = 0,
+  YCBCR444 = 1,
+  YCBCR422 = 2,
+  YCBCR420 = 3,
+};
+
+//! NV_OUTPUT_LINK: bits 0-7 the kind (1 TMDS, 2 TMDS scrambled, 3 FRL, 4 DisplayPort), bits 8-15
+//! the FRL rate (1..6), bits 16-23 the pixel format; 0, or no value: not said
+inline Link LinkOf(std::optional<uint64_t> value)
+{
+  if (!value)
+    return Link::UNKNOWN;
+  switch (*value & 0xff)
+  {
+    case 1:
+      return Link::TMDS;
+    case 2:
+      return Link::TMDS_SCRAMBLED;
+    case 3:
+      return Link::FRL;
+    case 4:
+      return Link::DP;
+    default:
+      return Link::UNKNOWN;
+  }
+}
+inline unsigned int FrlRateOf(std::optional<uint64_t> value)
+{
+  return value ? static_cast<unsigned int>((*value >> 8) & 0xff) : 0;
+}
+inline std::optional<Format> FormatOf(std::optional<uint64_t> value)
+{
+  if (!value || LinkOf(value) == Link::UNKNOWN)
+    return std::nullopt;
+  const unsigned int format = static_cast<unsigned int>((*value >> 16) & 0xff);
+  if (format > 3)
+    return std::nullopt;
+  return static_cast<Format>(format);
+}
+inline const char* Name(Link link)
+{
+  switch (link)
+  {
+    case Link::TMDS:
+      return "TMDS";
+    case Link::TMDS_SCRAMBLED:
+      return "TMDS, scrambled";
+    case Link::FRL:
+      return "FRL";
+    case Link::DP:
+      return "DisplayPort";
+    default:
+      return "not said by the driver";
+  }
+}
 
 /*!
- * \brief The TMDS character rate of a mode, in kHz: its pixel clock at bpc bits per component in
- * RGB or YCbCr 4:4:4 (8 at least). A mode sent in YCbCr 4:2:0 has half of it, which is not known
- * here: it is counted as at the full rate, which costs, at most, the output turned off and on
- * when that was not needed. A mode sent over FRL is above the limit too, and is set up again
- * the same way (its link is trained again).
+ * \brief The TMDS character rate of a mode, in kHz: its pixel clock at bpc bits per component (8
+ *        at least) in RGB or YCbCr 4:4:4; in 4:2:2 the pixel clock whatever the depth (up to 12
+ *        bits, carried as 8-bit RGB); in 4:2:0 half of the 4:4:4 rate. Where the format is not
+ *        known it is counted as RGB, which costs, at most, the output turned off and on when that
+ *        was not needed.
  */
-inline int64_t TmdsKHz(int64_t clockKHz, int bpc)
+inline int64_t TmdsKHz(int64_t clockKHz, int bpc, std::optional<Format> format = std::nullopt)
 {
-  return clockKHz * std::max(bpc, 8) / 8;
+  const int64_t full = clockKHz * std::max(bpc, 8) / 8;
+  if (format == Format::YCBCR422)
+    return clockKHz;
+  if (format == Format::YCBCR420)
+    return full / 2;
+  return full;
 }
 
-//! What the display's SCDC says of a link that the source scrambles
+//! What the display's SCDC says of the link that the source sends
 enum class Sink
 {
   UNKNOWN, //!< it could not be read
-  KEPT, //!< TMDS_Config has Scrambling_Enable and TMDS_Bit_Clock_Ratio on, as the source wrote them
+  KEPT, //!< the display has the setup that the source wrote
   LOST, //!< it has not (a sink that was switched off and on has 0)
 };
 
-//! What TMDS_Config says of a link above 340 MHz (as i915 compares it: intel_hdmi_reset_link())
-inline Sink SinkOf(std::optional<uint8_t> tmdsConfig)
+/*!
+ * \brief What the SCDC says: over FRL, FRL_Rate (Config_1, bits 3:0), which is 0 for TMDS and is
+ *        the rate that the link was trained at otherwise; over TMDS, TMDS_Config with
+ *        Scrambling_Enable and TMDS_Bit_Clock_Ratio both on (as i915 compares it:
+ *        intel_hdmi_reset_link()). TMDS_Config says nothing of an FRL link. Where the link is not
+ *        said, an FRL_Rate other than 0 is an FRL link that the display kept, and TMDS_Config is
+ *        read as for TMDS.
+ */
+inline Sink SinkOf(Link link, std::optional<uint8_t> tmdsConfig, std::optional<uint8_t> frlRate)
 {
+  if (link == Link::FRL)
+  {
+    if (!frlRate)
+      return Sink::UNKNOWN;
+    return (*frlRate & 0x0f) != 0 ? Sink::KEPT : Sink::LOST;
+  }
+  if (link == Link::UNKNOWN && frlRate && (*frlRate & 0x0f) != 0)
+    return Sink::KEPT;
   if (!tmdsConfig)
     return Sink::UNKNOWN;
   return (*tmdsConfig & 0x03) == 0x03 ? Sink::KEPT : Sink::LOST;
 }
 
 /*!
- * \brief The i2c adapter of the connector: the one whose DDC port gives the display's EDID (its
- *        first 128 bytes, as the EDID property of the connector has them). reads: each adapter
- *        that answered, with what it gave. -1 where none gives it, or more than one (two displays
- *        with the same EDID: which one is the connector's is not known).
+ * \brief That the adapter is the connector's: it gives the display's EDID (its first 128 bytes, as
+ *        the EDID property of the connector has them). reads: each adapter that answered, with what
+ *        it gave. -1 where none gives it, or more than one.
  */
 inline int AdapterOfEdid(const std::vector<std::pair<int, std::vector<uint8_t>>>& reads,
                          const std::vector<uint8_t>& edid)
@@ -123,6 +222,13 @@ inline int AdapterOfEdid(const std::vector<std::pair<int, std::vector<uint8_t>>>
   return found;
 }
 
+//! What a read of the display's SCDC found (made off the thread that draws), and what to log
+struct ScdcRead
+{
+  Sink sink = Sink::UNKNOWN;
+  std::string said;
+};
+
 enum class Verdict
 {
   NOT_DUE, //!< no hot-plug event since the last look
@@ -132,7 +238,7 @@ enum class Verdict
   NOT_HDMI, //!< the connector does not drive its display as HDMI now
   NOT_CONNECTED, //!< the display in use is not there now
   OFF, //!< the output is off (blanked, or meant to be off): its next turning on sets it up
-  NOT_SCRAMBLED, //!< at most 340 MHz: nothing to write again
+  NOT_SCRAMBLED, //!< a link with nothing written to the display (TMDS at 340 MHz or less)
   SINK_KEPT, //!< the display kept the setup that the driver wrote (SCDC)
   ECHO, //!< within AFTER_OWN_MS of the last time: the sink's answer to it
   OTHER_OUTPUT, //!< another output of the device changed and the one in use did not
@@ -150,13 +256,30 @@ struct Facts
   bool drivenAsHdmi = false; //!< NVIDIA_DUAL_MODE::DrivenAsHdmi()
   bool connected = false; //!< the display in use is there, as the kernel says after a probe
   bool on = false; //!< Kodi has the output on and the kernel says that its CRTC is active
-  int64_t tmdsKHz = 0; //!< TmdsKHz() of the mode on the output
+  Link link = Link::UNKNOWN; //!< what the driver says the link is (LinkOf())
+  int64_t tmdsKHz = 0; //!< TmdsKHz() of the mode on the output (where the link is not said)
   Sink sink = Sink::UNKNOWN; //!< what its SCDC says (read only where it can change the verdict)
   bool otherOutputChanged = false; //!< another output of the device changed since the last look
   int64_t sinceRelinkMs = -1; //!< since the output was last turned off and on for this; -1: never
   bool nativeDV = false; //!< native Dolby Vision is on (not the menus in Dolby Vision)
 };
 
+//! Whether the link has something written to the display that a display switched off and on loses
+inline bool SetUpLink(const Facts& f)
+{
+  switch (f.link)
+  {
+    case Link::TMDS_SCRAMBLED:
+    case Link::FRL:
+      return true;
+    case Link::TMDS:
+    case Link::DP:
+      return false;
+    default:
+      return f.tmdsKHz > SCRAMBLED_ABOVE_KHZ;
+  }
+}
+
 inline Verdict Decide(const Facts& f)
 {
   if (!f.due)
@@ -167,13 +290,13 @@ inline Verdict Decide(const Facts& f)
     return Verdict::SETTING_OFF;
   if (!f.nvidia)
     return Verdict::NOT_NVIDIA;
-  if (!f.drivenAsHdmi)
+  if (!f.drivenAsHdmi || f.link == Link::DP)
     return Verdict::NOT_HDMI;
   if (!f.connected)
     return Verdict::NOT_CONNECTED;
   if (!f.on)
     return Verdict::OFF;
-  if (f.tmdsKHz <= SCRAMBLED_ABOVE_KHZ)
+  if (!SetUpLink(f))
     return Verdict::NOT_SCRAMBLED;
   if (f.sink == Sink::KEPT)
     return Verdict::SINK_KEPT;
@@ -210,10 +333,10 @@ inline const char* Why(Verdict v)
     case Verdict::OFF:
       return "the output is off: turning it on sets the link up";
     case Verdict::NOT_SCRAMBLED:
-      return "the link of the mode is not scrambled (340 MHz or less): the display needs nothing "
-             "written again";
+      return "the link is neither scrambled nor trained (TMDS at 340 MHz or less): the display "
+             "needs nothing written again";
     case Verdict::SINK_KEPT:
-      return "the display kept the scrambling setup that the driver wrote";
+      return "the display kept the setup of the link that the driver wrote";
     case Verdict::ECHO:
       return "it came within 10 s of the output turned off and on for the event before: the "
              "display's answer to that";
@@ -229,7 +352,7 @@ inline const char* Why(Verdict v)
   }
 }
 
-//! Whether the verdict is told at INFO (the way that was taken where the link was scrambled)
+//! Whether the verdict is told at INFO (the way that was taken where the link was set up)
 inline bool TellsAtInfo(Verdict v)
 {
   return v == Verdict::SINK_KEPT || v == Verdict::ECHO || v == Verdict::OTHER_OUTPUT ||
diff --git a/xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp b/xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp
index 10303e3..b51e26d 100644
--- a/xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp
+++ b/xbmc/windowing/gbm/WinSystemGbmGLESContext.cpp
@@ -1310,26 +1310,36 @@ std::map<uint32_t, std::pair<bool, std::vector<uint8_t>>> OutputsOfDevice(int fd
   return outputs;
 }
 
-// Hades: the i2c adapters that the NVIDIA kernel module registers for the I2C ports of the GPU of a
-// DRM device (nv-i2c.c: "NVIDIA i2c adapter <port> at <bus>:<slot>.<function>", a child of the PCI
-// device; the adapters over DisplayPort AUX are named so too, and give no SCDC): their numbers, the
-// N of /dev/i2c-N
-std::vector<int> NvidiaI2cAdapters(int drmFd)
+// Hades: the PCI device of a DRM device, as sysfs has it ("" where it cannot be read)
+std::string PciDeviceOf(int drmFd)
 {
-  std::vector<int> adapters;
   struct stat st = {};
   if (fstat(drmFd, &st) != 0 || !S_ISCHR(st.st_mode))
-    return adapters;
+    return {};
   const std::string link = StringUtils::Format("/sys/dev/char/{}:{}/device", major(st.st_rdev),
                                                minor(st.st_rdev));
   char* real = realpath(link.c_str(), nullptr);
   if (!real)
-    return adapters;
-  const std::string device = real;
+    return {};
+  std::string device = real;
   free(real);
+  return device;
+}
+
+// Hades: the i2c adapter that the NVIDIA kernel module registers for one I2C port of the GPU of a
+// PCI device (nv-i2c.c: "NVIDIA i2c adapter <port> at <bus>:<slot>.<function>", a child of the PCI
+// device): its number, the N of /dev/i2c-N; -1 where there is none, or more than one. Only names
+// in sysfs are read: no bus is touched.
+int NvidiaI2cAdapter(const std::string& device, unsigned int port)
+{
+  if (device.empty())
+    return -1;
+  const std::string want = StringUtils::Format("NVIDIA i2c adapter {} at ", port);
   DIR* dir = opendir("/sys/bus/i2c/devices");
   if (!dir)
-    return adapters;
+    return -1;
+  int found = -1;
+  bool twice = false;
   while (const dirent* entry = readdir(dir))
   {
     int number = -1;
@@ -1343,18 +1353,18 @@ std::vector<int> NvidiaI2cAdapters(int drmFd)
     const std::string where = adapter;
     free(adapter);
     const auto slash = where.rfind('/');
-    if (slash == std::string::npos || where.compare(0, slash, device) != 0 ||
-        slash != device.size())
+    if (slash != device.size() || where.compare(0, slash, device) != 0)
       continue;
     std::ifstream file(path + "/name");
     std::string name;
     std::getline(file, name);
-    if (name.rfind("NVIDIA i2c adapter ", 0) == 0)
-      adapters.push_back(number);
+    if (name.rfind(want, 0) != 0)
